  "caveats": [
    "The gate metric is binary sign accuracy on |delta| >= 0.2091, the same convention as UP-2b and UP-23. Davidson's native metric is the 3-class accuracy, which is reported but is not the gate.",
    "The design is linear in the same features the Bradley-Terry arms use. A failure here is a failure of the linear pairwise class, not evidence that ties carry no information; the 3-class accuracy is the diagnostic that separates those two readings.",
    "delta is identified only up to the model's score scale, so its absolute value is interpretable within an arm and not across arms.",
    "The ligand-cluster bootstrap resamples ligands with replacement but keeps every pair of a resampled ligand, so within-ligand correlation is preserved and the interval is wider than a row-level bootstrap would be.",
    "tau is the metal pair's noise_sd_delta from msrs_metal_graph; a metal pair with no estimated edge falls back to tau=0, i.e. no deadzone, which is why the tau-sensitivity arms are not all on the same pair set.",
    "The pairwise fits are deterministic (ridge logistic, no sampling), so the five seeds produce identical scores and the seed axis carries no variance. The uncertainty quoted here is the ligand-cluster bootstrap, not a seed spread.",
    "On the gate-eligible pairs every outcome is a win or a loss by construction (|delta| >= 0.2091 >= tau), so the tie class is never the truth there. A model that predicts a tie on such a pair is wrong; the 3-class accuracy is reported to show how often Davidson does that, not as a competing headline."
  ]
